ROLE: evaluation_prosecutor  VOTE: yellow
MANDATORY_BASELINES: [{"name": "ROS 2 DDS (CycloneDDS/FastDDS) RELIABLE vs BEST_EFFORT", "why": "Zenoh is the default ROS 2 rmw replacement. Must compare how DDS handles slow-subscriber congestion (does it drop, or does it apply strict E2E backpressure?)", "repo_or_cite": "ROS 2 RMW specifications"}, {"name": "MQTT QoS 0 vs QoS 1 (e.g., Mosquitto)", "why": "Standard broker-based pub/sub. Establishes baseline for whether intermediate routers/brokers in pub/sub natively propagate backpressure E2E or drop at the queue boundary without explicit application-level ACKs.", "repo_or_cite": "MQTT 5.0 Specification"}]
STRAWMAN_RISKS: ["Expecting E2E backpressure from a decoupled pub/sub datagram push. In multi-hop routing, strict E2E publisher blocking would cause head-of-line blocking, degrading performance for all other fast subscribers. Framing per-hop buffering/dropping as a 'failure' rather than a deliberate architectural choice to prevent global distributed deadlock is a massive strawman unless Zenoh documentation explicitly promises E2E backpressure for 'put'."]
MISSING_METRICS: ["Impact on concurrent fast subscribers (does a slow sub degrade a fast sub?)", "Router queue memory saturation limits", "Comparison with Zenoh explicit query/RPC E2E mechanisms"]
KILLER_EXPERIMENT: <Deploy a 4-node topology: 1 Publisher -> 1 Router -> [1 Slow Subscriber + 1 Fast Subscriber]. If CongestionControl::Block were strictly end-to-end, the publisher would block, starving the fast subscriber of throughput. If the router drops for the slow sub but maintains high throughput for the fast sub, it proves the per-hop behavior is a deliberate feature to isolate congestion, falsifying the claim that it is a 'failure'.>
RATIONALE: The evaluation's 3-node topology successfully and rigorously isolates the per-hop nature of the `Block` knob. However, evaluating a multi-hop pub/sub router topology against expectations of TCP-like end-to-end backpressure ignores standard distributed systems architecture. E2E backpressure on a publisher often introduces catastrophic head-of-line blocking for the rest of the network. While the "split-default contract-flip" and "silence" (no logs/errors) are valid, actionable hazards that justify keeping this alive (preventing a RED vote), the claim that `Block` "measurably fails" end-to-end is evaluating an apple as an orange. The evaluation must run the 4-node killer experiment to determine if this per-hop behavior is actually an intentional routing isolation feature.
